Lab 1: Installing RHACM on Hub Cluster
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

• Install Red Hat Advanced Cluster Management (RHACM) on a hub OpenShift cluster using OperatorHub • Validate the health and status of RHACM components after installation • Verify the installation using both command-line tools and the web dashboard • Navigate the RHACM console and understand its key features • Troubleshoot common installation issues

Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

• Basic understanding of OpenShift Container Platform concepts • Familiarity with Kubernetes fundamentals (pods, services, deployments) • Experience using the oc command-line tool • Knowledge of OpenShift Operators and OperatorHub • Understanding of YAML configuration files • Basic Linux command-line skills

Required Access and Permissions
• Cluster administrator privileges on the OpenShift hub cluster • Access to the OpenShift web console • Command-line access to the cluster with oc CLI tool installed

Ready-to-Use Cloud Machines
Al Nafi provides pre-configured Linux-based cloud machines with all necessary tools installed. Simply click Start Lab to access your environment. No need to build your own virtual machine or install additional software.

Your lab environment includes: • OpenShift Container Platform cluster (hub cluster) • Pre-installed oc CLI tool • Web browser access to OpenShift console • All required network connectivity and permissions

Lab Tasks
Task 1: Prepare the Environment and Access the Hub Cluster
Subtask 1.1: Verify Cluster Access
First, let's ensure you have proper access to your OpenShift hub cluster.

Open a terminal in your lab environment

Verify your cluster connection:

oc whoami
Check cluster status:
oc get nodes
Verify you have cluster-admin privileges:
oc auth can-i '*' '*' --all-namespaces
Expected output should show yes, confirming administrative access.

Subtask 1.2: Check Cluster Resources
Before installing RHACM, verify your cluster has sufficient resources.

Check cluster version:
oc version
Verify node resources:
oc describe nodes
Check available storage classes:
oc get storageclass
Note: RHACM requires significant resources. Ensure your cluster has at least 8 CPU cores and 16GB RAM available across nodes.

Task 2: Install RHACM via OperatorHub
Subtask 2.1: Access OperatorHub in OpenShift Console
Open your web browser and navigate to the OpenShift console URL provided in your lab environment

Log in using your cluster administrator credentials

In the left navigation menu, click on Operators → OperatorHub

In the search box, type Advanced Cluster Management and press Enter

Click on the Advanced Cluster Management for Kubernetes tile

Subtask 2.2: Install the RHACM Operator
In the operator details page, click Install

Configure the installation settings: • Installation Mode: Select All namespaces on the cluster • Installed Namespace: Select openshift-operators • Update Channel: Choose release-2.9 (or latest stable version) • Approval Strategy: Select Automatic

Click Install to begin the operator installation

Wait for the installation to complete. You should see a green checkmark and "Succeeded" status.

Subtask 2.3: Verify Operator Installation via CLI
Return to your terminal and verify the operator installation:
oc get csv -n openshift-operators | grep advanced-cluster-management
Check operator pods:
oc get pods -n openshift-operators | grep advanced-cluster-management
Verify the operator is running:
oc get operators
Task 3: Create MultiClusterHub Instance
Subtask 3.1: Create the RHACM Namespace
Create a dedicated namespace for RHACM:
oc create namespace open-cluster-management
Verify namespace creation:
oc get namespace open-cluster-management
Subtask 3.2: Create MultiClusterHub Custom Resource
Create a YAML file for the MultiClusterHub configuration:
cat > multiclusterhub.yaml << EOF
apiVersion: operator.open-cluster-management.io/v1
kind: MultiClusterHub
metadata:
  name: multiclusterhub
  namespace: open-cluster-management
spec:
  availabilityConfig: High
  enableClusterBackup: false
  imagePullSecret: ""
  separateCertificateManagement: false
EOF
Apply the configuration:
oc apply -f multiclusterhub.yaml
Monitor the installation progress:
oc get multiclusterhub -n open-cluster-management -w
Note: The installation may take 10-15 minutes. Wait until the status shows Running.

Subtask 3.3: Alternative Method - Create via OpenShift Console
If you prefer using the web console:

Navigate to Operators → Installed Operators

Click on Advanced Cluster Management for Kubernetes

Click on the MultiClusterHub tab

Click Create MultiClusterHub

Use the default configuration or modify as needed

Click Create

Task 4: Validate Component Health
Subtask 4.1: Check MultiClusterHub Status
Verify the MultiClusterHub status:
oc get multiclusterhub -n open-cluster-management
Get detailed status information:
oc describe multiclusterhub multiclusterhub -n open-cluster-management
Check for any error conditions:
oc get multiclusterhub multiclusterhub -n open-cluster-management -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions}' | jq
Subtask 4.2: Verify RHACM Pods
List all pods in the RHACM namespace:
oc get pods -n open-cluster-management
Check pod status and ensure all are running:
oc get pods -n open-cluster-management --field-selector=status.phase!=Running
If this command returns no results, all pods are running correctly.

Check for any pods with restart counts:
oc get pods -n open-cluster-management -o wide
Subtask 4.3: Validate Services and Routes
Check RHACM services:
oc get services -n open-cluster-management
Verify routes are created:
oc get routes -n open-cluster-management
Get the RHACM console URL:
oc get route multicloud-console -n open-cluster-management -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}'
Task 5: Verify Installation with Dashboard Access
Subtask 5.1: Access RHACM Console
Get the RHACM console URL:
echo "https://$(oc get route multicloud-console -n open-cluster-management -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')"
Open this URL in your web browser

Log in using your OpenShift credentials

You should see the RHACM dashboard with the welcome screen

Subtask 5.2: Explore RHACM Dashboard Features
Navigate through the main sections: • Overview: Shows cluster summary and status • Infrastructure: Manage clusters and providers • Applications: Application lifecycle management • Governance: Policy and compliance management

Click on Infrastructure → Clusters to see your hub cluster listed

Verify the hub cluster shows as "Ready" status

Subtask 5.3: Validate Installation via CLI Commands
Check RHACM version:
oc get multiclusterhub multiclusterhub -n open-cluster-management -o jsonpath='{.status.currentVersion}'
List all RHACM-related custom resource definitions:
oc get crd | grep cluster-management
Verify cluster management add-ons:
oc get managedclusteraddons -A
Check the hub cluster registration:
oc get managedclusters
You should see your hub cluster listed as "local-cluster".

Task 6: Post-Installation Configuration and Testing
Subtask 6.1: Configure RBAC (Optional)
Create a sample user with RHACM access:
cat > rhacm-user-rbac.yaml << EOF
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: rhacm-admin-binding
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: open-cluster-management:cluster-manager-admin
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: rhacm-admin
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
EOF
Apply the RBAC configuration:
oc apply -f rhacm-user-rbac.yaml
Subtask 6.2: Test Basic Functionality
Create a test namespace to verify RHACM is working:
oc create namespace rhacm-test
Label the namespace for RHACM management:
oc label namespace rhacm-test cluster.open-cluster-management.io/managedCluster=local-cluster
Verify the label was applied:
oc get namespace rhacm-test --show-labels
Clean up the test namespace:
oc delete namespace rhacm-test
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Operator Installation Fails
Symptoms: Operator shows "Failed" status in OperatorHub

Solution:

# Check operator logs
oc logs -n openshift-operators deployment/advanced-cluster-management-operator

# Verify subscription status
oc get subscription -n openshift-operators
Issue 2: MultiClusterHub Stuck in Pending
Symptoms: MultiClusterHub remains in "Pending" state

Solution:

# Check events for errors
oc get events -n open-cluster-management --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'

# Verify resource quotas
oc describe quota -n open-cluster-management
Issue 3: Pods Not Starting
Symptoms: RHACM pods show "Pending" or "CrashLoopBackOff"

Solution:

# Check pod details
oc describe pod <pod-name> -n open-cluster-management

# Check node resources
oc top nodes

# Verify storage classes
oc get pvc -n open-cluster-management
Issue 4: Console Access Problems
Symptoms: Cannot access RHACM console

Solution:

# Verify route exists
oc get route multicloud-console -n open-cluster-management

# Check route status
oc describe route multicloud-console -n open-cluster-management

# Verify console pod is running
oc get pods -n open-cluster-management | grep console
Validation Checklist
Before completing this lab, ensure the following items are verified:

 RHACM Operator is installed and running
 MultiClusterHub custom resource is in "Running" status
 All RHACM pods are in "Running" state with 0 restarts
 RHACM console is accessible via web browser
 Hub cluster appears as "local-cluster" in managed clusters
 No error events in the open-cluster-management namespace
 All required services and routes are created
 RHACM version information is displayed correctly
